        "number": 1,
        "instruction": "Season beef and pork mixture liberally with salt. Combine the meats gently without overmixing, then form into balls about the size of golf balls. Do not worry about even distribution.",
        "duration": "5 min",
        "tips": "Forming into balls allows you to brown all the meat in just two batches with excellent color development on the outside without overcooking the interior. The meat will cook through completely during the braising process.",
        "timestamp": 60
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "instruction": "Heat a large heavy-bottomed pot or Dutch oven over medium-high heat. Once hot, add the meatballs in a single batch (about half). Let them sit undisturbed for 2–3 minutes to develop color, then turn occasionally until deeply browned on all sides. Transfer to a plate and repeat with the remaining meatballs.",
        "duration": "10 min",
        "tips": "Do not move the meatballs around constantly — you want maximum contact with the hot surface to develop flavor. Do not scorch the fond (brown bits) at the bottom of the pan; that's where all the flavor lives. The gaps between balls allow steam to escape, preventing stewing.",
        "timestamp": 141
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "instruction": "Add pancetta to the same pot (do not clean it). Cook over medium heat, stirring occasionally, until lightly browned and fat is rendered, about 3–4 minutes.",
        "duration": "4 min",
        "tips": "The cured pork imparts massive amounts of flavor to the sauce. Do not let the pot get too hot before adding the pancetta — you want gentle rendering, not scorching.",
        "timestamp": 221
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "instruction": "Add diced onion, carrot, and celery to the pancetta. Season lightly with salt. Increase heat slightly and cook, stirring often, until vegetables soften and their color intensifies, about 5–7 minutes.",
        "duration": "7 min",
        "tips": "Salt helps draw moisture from the vegetables and speeds up cooking. Carrot and celery give the ragù a well-rounded base of vegetable aromatics — they belong in bolognese. Do not brown the vegetables; just soften them.",
        "timestamp": 280
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "instruction": "Push vegetables to the side of the pot. Add Mutti brand double-concentrated tomato paste (from the tube) directly onto the vegetables. Let it sit for 1 minute, then stir to combine with the oil and rendered fat. Cook for 1–2 minutes until the paste darkens slightly.",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "The tomato paste must go on top of the aromatics so it can cook out at a higher temperature than it would in liquid. This concentrates its flavor. Do not add liquid first — the oil will fry the paste and intensify it.",
        "timestamp": 341
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "instruction": "Pour in 1 cup of red wine. Stir well. Let the wine simmer gently, stirring occasionally, for 8–10 minutes until it reduces by half and the alcohol cooks off. The sauce should smell concentrated and rich.",
        "duration": "10 min",
        "tips": "The wine adds acidity and complexity. Cook it down on its own so the alcohol burns off completely. You should see the fat begin to separate from the liquid as it concentrates.",
        "timestamp": 401
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "instruction": "Add the hand-crushed canned tomatoes and stir well. Increase heat to medium and simmer for about 8–10 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the sauce looks almost like it's frying as much as it's simmering. The fat should appear to separate slightly from the liquid.",
        "duration": "10 min",
        "tips": "Do not worry if the sauce tastes bright at this point — it will balance out in the oven with the meat. Crush the tomatoes by hand for even texture; ensure you have clean cuts with no jagged edges.",
        "timestamp": 442
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "instruction": "Add 1 cup low-sodium chicken broth and 1 cup whole milk to the tomato mixture. Stir well to combine. Return the meatballs to the pot, nestling them into the sauce. Increase heat slightly and bring to a gentle simmer.",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "The milk adds creaminess and helps balance the acidity of the tomatoes — this is traditional for ragù bolognese. Do not skip it. Get the sauce back to a simmer before moving to the oven.",
        "timestamp": 482
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "instruction": "Cover the pot with a lid slightly ajar. Transfer to a 225°F oven. Braise for 3–4 hours, or until the meat has broken down completely and the sauce is dark, rich, and has reduced by about one-third. Check occasionally and stir if needed.",
        "duration": "180 min",
        "tips": "The low, slow cooking allows all the flavors to marry and the meat to become tender and almost disintegrate into the sauce. The sauce freezes beautifully for months, so this step can be done well in advance.",
        "timestamp": 520
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "instruction": "While the ragù braises, make the béchamel: Melt 6 tablespoons butter in a saucepan over medium heat. Once fully melted, add 7 tablespoons all-purpose flour, whisking constantly for 1 minute to create a smooth paste (roux). Continue cooking for another minute to toast the flour.",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "Toasting the flour develops incredible flavor — do not skip this step. The roux should bubble gently but not brown. This flavor development is what makes béchamel special in lasagna.",
        "timestamp": 519
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "instruction": "Gradually add 4 cups whole milk to the roux, whisking vigorously and constantly as you pour to prevent lumps. Increase heat to medium-high. Continue whisking until the sauce comes to a boil.",
        "duration": "5 min",
        "tips": "Whisk hard to avoid lumps. Do not worry about splashing — it's part of the process. The starch must come to a boil to fully thicken the sauce, so the sooner you reach a boil, the better.",
        "timestamp": 540
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "instruction": "Once boiling, reduce heat to medium-low and simmer, whisking occasionally, until the béchamel thickens to the consistency of a thick gravy, about 10 minutes. You should see it coat the back of a spoon.",
        "duration": "10 min",
        "tips": "Keep whisking occasionally to prevent lumps and ensure even thickening. The sauce will continue to thicken slightly as it cools.",
        "timestamp": 560
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "instruction": "Remove béchamel from heat. Stir in 2 cups grated Parmigiano-Reggiano, 1/4 teaspoon freshly grated nutmeg, and a pinch of cayenne pepper. Taste and adjust seasoning with salt and black pepper.",
        "duration": "2 min",
        "tips": "Nutmeg with béchamel is traditional and essential — it rounds out the sauce. Cayenne adds just a subtle lift without making the dish spicy; it wakes up the flavors.",
        "timestamp": 623
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "instruction": "Bring a large pot of salted water to a rolling boil. Working in two batches to prevent sticking, carefully add lasagna sheets one at a time, stirring and agitating gently as they go in. Cook until very al dente and flexible but still with a slight snap when bent, about 3 minutes.",
        "duration": "8 min",
        "tips": "Lasagna sheets stick together easily — add them one at a time and stir constantly. Do not overcook them; they should be flexible with a slight snap to them. Two batches prevents overcrowding.",
        "timestamp": 580
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "instruction": "Remove cooked pasta sheets and lay them out individually between pieces of parchment paper (or wax paper). Do not stack them on top of each other or leave them in water too long, or they will stick together and absorb too much water.",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "If a couple of sheets stick together, do not panic — just carefully separate them and move on. The parchment prevents sticking and keeps the sheets from drying out.",
        "timestamp": 600
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "instruction": "When the ragù has finished braising (meat broken down, sauce dark and reduced), remove it from the oven and let cool for 5 minutes. Using a wooden spoon, break up the remaining meatballs into smaller pieces and stir to integrate the solid and liquid elements of the sauce. The sauce should be thick but still loose enough to pour.",
        "duration": "5 min",
        "tips": "Lightly break up the meatballs — you want some texture, not a completely smooth sauce. A thin layer of oil on top prevents sticking during storage.",
        "timestamp": 743
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "instruction": "Preheat oven to 425°F. Choose a 9x13-inch (or similar) baking dish. Lightly oil the bottom. Spread a thin layer of ragù on the bottom (about 1/4 cup).",
        "duration": "2 min",
        "tips": "A thin bottom layer prevents sticking. The layering strategy is: sauce, pasta, béchamel, sauce, pasta, béchamel — five thin layers are better than three thick ones for even texture.",
        "timestamp": 795
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "instruction": "Lay down pasta sheets in a single layer, slightly overlapping and covering the bottom. Trim sheets as needed using d'czecho's wider format for easier coverage. Spread a thin layer of ragù over the pasta (about 1/3 cup), then a thin layer of béchamel (about 1/4 cup).",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "D'czecho sheets are wider and more forgiving — you can easily trim strips to fill gaps. Vary the direction of each layer so pasta pieces interlock and overlap for better structural integrity. Thin layers cook more evenly.",
        "timestamp": 840
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "instruction": "Repeat the layering process: pasta, ragù, béchamel. Continue until you reach the top of the dish, ending with a final layer of pasta sheets, then a layer of béchamel on top. Reserve a small amount of ragù to drizzle over the very top if desired.",
        "duration": "5 min",
        "tips": "Aim for 5 layers total — each component gets a thin, even distribution. The final béchamel layer on top will brown beautifully and create a creamy barrier. Do not make layers too thick or the lasagna will not heat through evenly.",
        "timestamp": 900
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "instruction": "Cover the baking dish loosely with aluminum foil (do not let it touch the béchamel layer). Transfer to the 425°F oven. Bake for 25 minutes covered. Remove foil and bake for an additional 10–15 minutes, or until the top is golden brown and bubbling at the edges.",
        "duration": "40 min",
        "tips": "Covering with foil prevents the top from browning too quickly while the interior heats through. Remove the foil near the end so the top can crisp up and turn golden. Listen for gentle bubbling at the edges — that's your signal it's done.",
        "timestamp": 940
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "instruction": "Remove lasagna from the oven and let rest, undisturbed, for 10 minutes before serving. This allows all the liquid and layers to rebalance.",
        "duration": "10 min",
        "tips": "Do not skip this resting period — it is essential for clean, sturdy slices that hold together. The lasagna will be extremely hot when it comes out; resting prevents you from burning your mouth and gives the sauce time to set slightly.",
        "timestamp": 940
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "instruction": "Using a sharp knife or spatula, carefully cut the lasagna into portions. Serve immediately, spooning any extra ragù and béchamel from the pan over the top. Finish with a pinch of flaky salt if desired.",
        "duration": null,
        "tips": "A clean, sharp knife yields the best-looking slices. Corner pieces are extra crispy because the edges brown more — they are the prize. The béchamel on the exposed pasta edges will crisp up like a cracker.",
